@X0BIntroduction to multiport cards-
--------------------------------
@X03There are 2 general types of multiport cards.  There are intelligent cards,
which have an onboard risc processor.  The advantage to these cards is that
they will take overhead off the machine.  Non-intelligent cards are simpler.
They can be installed in a similar manner as with a standard serial card.
 
@X0BIntelligent Cards-
------------------
@X03Most intelligent cards have the ability to use just one IRQ all ports on the
card.  In some cards such as GTEK, they are able to share an IRQ between
several "daisy-chained" cards.
 
Under DOS, you can use a program called COMMDRV.  This program supports both
intelligent and non-intelligent (also called dumb) cards.
 
Type @X02DRVSETUP COMMDRV.DAT@X03.  Some cards, such as DIGI 8e and 4e have a .DAT file
already set up for you.  Substitute that file for commdrv.dat.  Ex.
@X02DRVSETUP DIGI4E.DAT@X03.  If the .DAT file is not set up already, then follow these
steps:
 
@X091) Press F2 under @X03CARD TYPE@X09.
   Select card type.  For DIGI cards, the selection is often PC/XI (including
   the 4e and 8e cards.)  If your card is digi compatible or is not on the
   list, try this selection.  Other card types are fairly straight forward on
   the list.
 
2) In the next field, select the @X03SUB PORT@X09.
   The sub port is the port number that you want your card to START with.  For
   example, If you have a standard serial card with com1 and com2, you'll want
   a 4 port card to start on com3.  @X04
   NOTE: The port number is always at least 1 higher than the CHANNEL number,
   since the port numbers start at 1 and the channel numbers start at 0.
 
@X093) In the @X03BASE ADDRESS @X09field, select an address that IS NOT OCCUPIED.  If the
memory address is occupied by another program or hardware, then the  card will
fail to initialize or operate properly.  The base address that you select must
ahve the next 64k free.  The base address may be the same for all ports on an
intelligent card.  With some cards, the base address should increment for each
port by 8h.  The base address used should be specified with a jumper setting
on the card.  The IRQ can be specified as any IRQ that you may have free.
The IRQ need not be specified by a jumper setting on the card.  The onboard
computer can autodetect the IRQ.  Intelligent cards also have a memory region
that they use.  This region is specified in the @X03CARD SEGMENT@X09 field in the
commdrv setup.  The default for many intelligent cards is D000h.  In the @X03FOSSIL @X09
field, specify whether or not you want COMMDRV to provide a fossil interface
for you.  A fossil driver is often necessary under OS/2 or windows95/NT for
doors, zmodem transfers or QWK transfers.  At this point your commdrv is set
up.
 
@X03
You will need to make a change to your config.sys file.  The expanded memory
manager you are using must exclude your card segment address.
 
You are ready to install your commdrv setup.  From the command line (in your
AUTOEXEC.BAT) type:
@X07COMMTSR -i COMMDRV.DAT
@X03Notice that the -i is in lower case.. this is necessary.
Save your changes, and then reboot your computer.

If you are running an Intelligent card under OS/2, you'll need OS/2 drivers for
that card from the maker of the card.  These are not always available, so use
caution when buying an intelligent card for OS/2.  GTEK, ARNET, DIGI, COMMTROL
all come have OS/2 SERIAL drivers, but only GTEK also has a FOSSIL driver.
Again, this is important.  The installation for these serial/fossil drivers is
dependant on the driver and the card.  Most of the time, they'll both be
installed in the config.sys.


@X0BNON-INTELLIGENT CARDS-
----------------------
@X03Dumb cards can be specified as UART type cards.  They are capable of sharing
IRQs between ports and (in some models) daisy chained cards.  There is no
card segment and no memory region to exclude from EMM386 or QEMM.  The base
address should increment by 8h with ALL cards.
 
As is the case with intelligent cards, the memory address specified in the
base address MUST NOT be occupied by any other application or hardware.
 
COMMDRV can be used under DOS, Win95 can use it's own drivers, and OS/2 can
use it's default serial driver, but will not be able to support more than 4
ports and will not support Fossil.  You can use drivers that are specific to
the card (from the maker of the card), and you'll have more luck.
 
Another alternative under OS/2 is SIO.SYS by Ray Gwinn.  The shareware version
of this driver is available on Salt Air - @X0CSIO153.ZIP@X03.  To install SIO153.ZIP,
just type INSTALL from the directory where you unzipped it.  It will install
SIO.SYS and VSIO.SYS (which are both necessary and work in conjunction with
each other) in your config.sys.  It will ask you about if you're using a Hayes
ESP card.  If you have purchased  Hayes ESP 2 or 8 port multiserial port card
(you'll know if you have), then enter YES.  If not, enter NO.


@X0BSEMI-INTELLIGENT CARDS-
-----------------------

@X03The Hayes ESP card is a semi-intelligent card.  These cards have on onboard
risc processor like an intelligent card, but they can be accessed as a UART.
This is why SIO can access the card.  The advantage to this card is that it
will take the overhead off the computer, and can be accessed by almost any
generic non-intelligent driver which makes it easy to provide a fossil
interface for the card.  The disadvantage is that it is difficult to use
under any operating system other than OS/2.

